{"parentUuid":"3c540894-6079-476e-ab07-45309c519de7","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CgNTdQLPvBJxMcDJrrW4M","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I'm blocked by an infrastructure failure, so here's the precise state.\n\n**Done and pushed:** #489 (`c0117710`, partial-clone guard), #478 (`67d3321c`), #477 (`906e8ad3`).\n\n**#483 is resolved and verified but not committed.** Its agent did the work, then the Bash safety classifier started erroring — no verdict, repeatedly — so it couldn't run `git commit`. I've hit the same failure about six times and stopped retrying rather than burn the turn. This is an infrastructure fault, not a permission denial and not a problem with the code.\n\nI verified what I could read-only: `MERGE_HEAD` is `ab575071`, so the merge really is in progress, and `history.go:1433-1434` shows the correct resolution — main's `clibase.MaxDocumentTranscriptBytes` with the branch's `historyTranscriptTooLargeError` sentinel kept, no conflict markers. The agent's full gate passed on that tree (EXIT=0, 20 packages, a weakening red).\n\nI wrote `/Users/suhaan/devenv/RESUME-483-UNCOMMITTED.md` with the exact commands, the resolution rationale, and the evidence, so nothing is lost if this session ends.\n\nTwo things I verified rather than assumed, both of which could have gone unnoticed:\n\n- #477's agent flagged that it hadn't checked the cap-before-allocation ordering. I read it: negative refused at 255, cap at 258, `make` at 261 — intact, and the `math.MaxInt` case that makes the *ordering* load-bearing survived the merge.\n- Both #477 and #478 conflicted in the same file over the same per-branch code, which looked like an unreported stack. It isn't — #478 is **not** an ancestor of #477, so the two-stack merge plan I gave the graph session stands.\n\nStill yours: Approvals, transcript mode, the 37 GB delete. Still mine, when Bash recovers: #483's commit, then re-pushing #493 (it holds a 